Q: LINQ object aggregation A: <p>The common usage is to modify the accumulator and then return it, not to create a new object. In this case, I'd expect to see the following code:</p>

<pre><code>var aggregatedObject = myObjects.Aggregate(new MyCoolObject(),
    (accumulator, next) =&gt; {
       accumulator.Value1 +=next.Value1;
       accumulator.Value2 +=next.Value2;
       return accumulator;
    });
</code></pre>

<p>Returning a new object from the accumulating function makes sense only if modifying the accumulator has side effects. A good accumulator object though should <em>not</em> produce side effects. In such a case it would be better to use a different object type as the accumulator.</p>
